<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="course-06">
  <title>Group theory</title>
  <section xml:id="course-06-lesson-01">
    <title>Groups and subgroups</title>
    <p>The abstraction of symmetry begins with a set of operations that can be composed and undone.</p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>A group is a set <m>G</m> with an associative binary operation, an identity <m>e</m>, and an inverse for every element. A subgroup <m>H</m> is a subset that is itself a group under the restricted operation.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example>
      <p>The integers under addition form a group; nonzero real numbers under multiplication form another. Invertible matrices form <m>\operatorname{GL}(n,k)</m>, where multiplication generally does not commute. The natural numbers under addition do not form a group because positive elements lack inverses.</p>
    </example>
    <proposition>
      <title>subgroup test</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A nonempty subset <m>H</m> of <m>G</m> is a subgroup if and only if <m>ab^{-1}</m> belongs to <m>H</m> for all a,b in <m>H</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Necessity follows from closure and inverses. Conversely choose <m>h</m> in <m>H</m>. Then <m>hh^{-1}=e</m> lies in <m>H</m>. Taking <m>a=e</m> gives <m>b^{-1}</m> in <m>H</m>. Applying the condition to a and <m>b^{-1}</m> gives ab in <m>H</m>. Associativity is inherited.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <remark>
      <p>The subgroup test condenses three checks into one. Nonemptiness is essential: the empty set satisfies the quantified closure condition vacuously.</p>
    </remark>
    <exercises>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Show that the center <m>Z(G)</m> is a subgroup.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>The identity commutes with every element. If a,b commute with every <m>g</m>, then <m>b^{-1}</m> does too, so <m>ab^{-1}</m> commutes with every <m>g</m>. Apply the subgroup test.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="course-06-lesson-02">
    <title>Cyclic groups</title>
    <p>A cyclic group is the simplest kind of group: all elements arise by repeatedly applying one generator.</p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>For <m>g</m> in <m>G</m>, the cyclic subgroup generated by <m>g</m> is <m>\langle g\rangle =\{g^{n}</m> : <m>n\in \mathbb Z \}</m>. A group is cyclic if <m>G=\langle g\rangle</m>. The order of <m>g</m> is the least positive <m>n</m> with <m>g^{n}=e</m>, or infinity if none exists.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example>
      <p>In <m>\mathbb Z /12\mathbb Z</m>, the element 8 generates {0,8,4}. Its order is 3. The element 5 generates the whole group.</p>
    </example>
    <theorem>
      <title>classification</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Every cyclic group is isomorphic to <m>\mathbb Z</m> or <m>\mathbb Z /n\mathbb Z</m> for a unique positive integer <m>n</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>The homomorphism <m>\phi :\mathbb Z \to G</m>, <m>m\mapsto g^{m}</m>, is surjective. If <m>g</m> has infinite order its kernel is zero. Otherwise let <m>n</m> be the order. Division with remainder shows that <m>g^{m}=e</m> exactly when <m>n</m> divides <m>m</m>. Hence <m>\operatorname{ker}</m> <m>\phi =n\mathbb Z</m>, and the first isomorphism theorem yields the claim. A direct proof identifies residue classes with powers of <m>g</m>.</p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <proposition>
      <title>order of a power</title>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>g</m> has finite order <m>n</m>, the order of <m>g^{k}</m> is <m>n/\operatorname{gcd}(n,k)</m>. Indeed <m>(g^{k})^{m}=e</m> exactly when <m>n</m> divides km. Writing <m>n=dn</m>′ and <m>k=dk</m>′ with <m>\operatorname{gcd}(n</m>′,k′<m>)=1</m> reduces this to <m>n</m>′ dividing <m>m</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <theorem>
      <title>subgroups</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Every subgroup of a cyclic group is cyclic. For a nontrivial subgroup choose the least positive <m>d</m> with <m>g^{d}</m> in <m>H</m>; division with remainder shows every exponent occurring in <m>H</m> is divisible by <m>d</m>. In the finite case <m>d</m> divides <m>n</m>, and <m>H</m> has order <m>n/d</m>. The trivial subgroup is generated by <m>e</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <exercises>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Find all generators and subgroups of <m>\mathbb Z /12\mathbb Z</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Generators are 1,5,7,11. Subgroups are generated by 1,2,3,4,6,12, with respective orders 12,6,4,3,2,1.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
