  <section xml:id="course-06-lesson-05">
    <title>Normal subgroups and quotients</title>
    <p>Quotients systematically declare certain elements indistinguishable while preserving the group operation.</p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p><m>N</m> is normal in <m>G</m> if <m>gNg^{-1}=N</m> for every <m>g\in G</m>. The quotient <m>G/N</m> consists of left cosets gN with multiplication <m>(gN)(hN)=ghN</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem>
      <title>well-definedness</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Coset multiplication is well-defined exactly when <m>N</m> is normal.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>If <m>g</m>′<m>=gn</m> and <m>h</m>′<m>=hm</m> with <m>n,m\in N</m>, then <m>g</m>′<m>h</m>′<m>=gh(h^{-1}nh)m</m> lies in ghN by normality. Conversely, well-defined multiplication implies <m>(gN)(N)(g^{-1}N)=N</m>, hence <m>gng^{-1}\in N</m> for every <m>n\in N</m>. Applying this also to <m>g^{-1}</m> gives equality.</p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <example>
      <p>The sign map <m>S_{3}\to \{\pm 1\}</m> has kernel <m>A_{3}</m>. Thus <m>S_{3}/A_{3}</m> is cyclic of order two. By contrast a subgroup generated by a transposition is not normal because conjugation sends it to a different transposition subgroup.</p>
    </example>
    <remark>
      <title>Universal property</title>
      <p>If <m>f:G\to K</m> is a homomorphism with <m>N</m> contained in <m>\operatorname{ker}</m> <m>f</m>, there is a unique homomorphism <m>G/N\to K</m> sending gN to <m>f(g)</m>. It is well-defined because <m>f(gn)=f(g)</m>.</p>
    </remark>
    <exercises>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Prove every subgroup of index two is normal.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>For <m>g</m> outside <m>N</m>, both gN and Ng equal the complement of <m>N</m>; for <m>g</m> in <m>N</m> both equal <m>N</m>. Thus left and right cosets coincide.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
